WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.240
ROGER MARTINEZ: Hey, Roger
here from Google Cloud.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.900
And in this video,
we'll have a look

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.120
at how developers can
extend G Suite to build

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.910
seamless solutions enabling
users of popular tools

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.820
like Gmail, Chat, and Drive
to work faster, easier,

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:13.920
and smarter.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.660
Specifically, we'll
look at an end

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.760
to end solution that
highlights newer developer

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.280
technologies like
G Suite add-ons,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.090
Chatbots, and accelerated
mobile pages for email.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.220
By showcasing different
types of integrations

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.110
that developers can
build with G Suite,

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.730
we hope to inspire
you to build your own.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.340
So let's get started.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.920
Meet Anu, the manager
of a customer service

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.590
team at another corp.
She receives an email

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.110
from one of her
customers, AltoStrat,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.420
who wants help resolving
a nagging issue,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.910
inconsistent access
to the payment portal.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.300
Obviously, resolving this
in a timely manner is key.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.100
In the email from
the customer, we

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.920
can see a couple of key pieces
of information, the issue

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.660
number, and, of course,
the customer name.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.120
As a developer, you can
leverage that information

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.060
in the context of the email
to build applications that

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.160
can really benefit the manager.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.110
Here, we can see
how Anu launches her

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.060
CRM add-on in Gmail.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.190
Without having to
switch applications, log

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.980
into another system, and
risk losing focus and flow,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.510
she can quickly
triage the customer

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.780
to see important details such
as key contacts and the type

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.910
of service contract
that they have.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.820
Next, still within the
CRM add-on in Gmail,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.010
Anu can see that
AltoStrat is also

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.110
an important sales
prospect with a couple

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.060
of big deals on the table.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.200
Now that Anu understands
the customer better,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.410
it's time to dig in on the
issue that they're having.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.777
Anu has another add-on
available in Gmail

00:01:36.777 --> 00:01:38.610
where she can quickly
understand the history

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.330
and status of the issue.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.930
Note, the add-on used the
context of the issue number

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.410
from the email so Anu didn't
have to retype it or look it up

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.550
in another system.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.590
With a closer
look, Anu discovers

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.510
that the issue had
been open in the past,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.380
but was not yet assigned to
a technician to resolve it.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.060
In order to get
this back on track,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.280
Anu takes action to
escalate the issue

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.170
and receive immediate
attention by changing

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.010
its priority to a critical
P0 right within the add-on.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.919
Anu can also add an explanation
of the changes in a custom

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:08.419
comment.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.710
Now she needs to assign
someone to cover the issue.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.380
Given the urgency, sending
out an email to the team

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.000
may not be fast enough.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.430
So, while still
within Gmail, Anu

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.070
switches over to using Chat
to quickly reach everyone

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.730
in the customer care
team via their chat room.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.620
Anu invokes the
team's Kanban Chatbot

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.220
with a simple at mention.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.850
And the custom built
chatbot responds

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.210
to everyone's availability,
pulling from the team's

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.370
resource planning back end app.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:42.940
Quickly, Anu new sees that
Guru is not fully tasked

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.190
and pings him directly,
asking if he can help.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.110
Once he confirms
that he can, Anu

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.110
uses another custom chat bot
to assign the task directly

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.900
to Guru from within the
conversation, making sure

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.980
that it's immediately
assigned and recorded.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.620
Now that we've seen how Anu
was able to triage and assign

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.230
the issue, let's take a look
at how our customer service

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.900
rep can use G Suite extensions
to help resolve the customer

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:14.472
issue.

00:03:14.472 --> 00:03:15.930
Now that the task
has been assigned

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.920
to Guru, when he
checks his inbox,

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.100
he sees the email notifying
him of the assignment.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.280
However, the email itself
is a little unique.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.050
As you can see here by the
lightning bolt designation,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.290
it's a dynamic email,
which is using the AMP

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.420
for Email spec in Gmail.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.980
What makes this
interesting is Guru

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.215
can interact with this
email much like he would

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:36.610
the issue tracking application.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:40.450
But once again, without
having to leave his inbox.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.820
For example, he can browse
the details of the issue,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.430
see related documents
that might be useful,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:52.570
and even review the history
of the issue up to that point.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.250
Guru can quickly
see the comments

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.840
the manager added
when escalating

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.490
this issue for the customer.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.203
Then, right inside
the email, Guru

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:01.120
can acknowledge that he
is accepting the issue

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.670
being assigned to him, saving
time and allowing the team

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.045
to know that he's
got it covered.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.580
The power of the dynamic
email becomes very apparent.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.240
As you can see,
Guru's acceptance

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.780
is instantly displayed in
the body and the activity

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.329
history of the original email.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:19.519
There's even an
inline UI in the email

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:21.200
where Guru can leave
his own comment

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.780
where he logs that he has a
good idea of what the issue is.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.180
When the issue tracking
generated this dynamic email,

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.520
it also added a folder
and provided a link

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.060
to the resolution template
using the Drive API,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.255
saving the customer service
rep time in locating documents

00:04:38.255 --> 00:04:40.130
while ensuring they use
the correct templates

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.710
and store them
correctly within Drive.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.447
Now let's fast
forward to when Guru

00:04:44.447 --> 00:04:47.030
has worked through the problem
and wants to record the process

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.920
and close out the issue.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.140
The customer service
team Guru works in stores

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.920
all customer documents in a
shared drive on Google Drive.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.540
It's here where
Guru will wrap up

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:00.420
the process of capturing steps
and outcomes of the issue.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.180
But like we saw
before with Gmail

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.500
and how you can leverage
add-ons with contexts,

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.340
you can do the same with
Google Drive as well.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.140
The same add-on
written and published

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.720
once works across all G Suite.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.440
So after a quick scan of the doc
to make sure it's all complete,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.300
Guru can access
the add-on in Drive

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.340
and change the status
to closed without ever

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.230
having to leave G Suite.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.130
And that's it.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.900
Problem solved.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.210
As you can see, G Suite
enables developers

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.300
to provide solutions that can
integrate complex processes

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.660
and data seamlessly
into G Suite.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.230
The solutions you can build
can reduce contact switching,

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:38.340
improve workflow, and make
it easier for users and teams

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.970
to get things done.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.970
If you'd like to learn
more, check out the links

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.097
in the description below.

00:05:43.097 --> 00:05:45.180
And don't forget, subscribe
to our YouTube channel

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.400
for more great content.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.150
